   ```
   Calling `removed.add(...)` or returning it causes a compile-time error. It should be initialized as a new `ArrayList`.

2. **Uses `==` instead of `<=`**
   ```java
   getGradYear() == year
   ```
   Members from any earlier graduation year must also be removed.

3. **Does not check good standing**

   The returned list should contain only graduates who are in good standing. All graduates should be removed from `memberList`, but members not in good standing should not be added to the returned list.

4. **Removing while advancing the index can skip members**

   After removing the element at index `i`, the next element shifts into that position. Incrementing `i` may cause the next member to be skipped. An `Iterator` safely supports removal during iteration.

### Correct implementation

```java
public ArrayList<MemberInfo> removeMembers(int year) {
    ArrayList<MemberInfo> removed = new ArrayList<MemberInfo>();
    Iterator<MemberInfo> iterator = memberList.iterator();

    while (iterator.hasNext()) {
        MemberInfo member = iterator.next();

        if (member.getGradYear() <= year) {
            if (member.inGoodStanding()) {
                removed.add(member);
            }

            iterator.remove();
        }
    }

    return removed;
}
```

This removes every member whose graduation year is at most `year`, while returning only those members who are in good standing. If no members qualify, `removed` is returned as an empty list.

